1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,110
That 6000 speed on your RAM box?

2
00:00:03,390 --> 00:00:05,671
Your PC probably isn't using it.

3
00:00:06,421 --> 00:00:11,044
New memory starts at a safe default speed set by an industry standard.

4
00:00:11,324 --> 00:00:15,470
For DDR5, that's usually 4800.

5
00:00:15,750 --> 00:00:20,162
For DDR4, it's often 2133.

6
00:00:20,912 --> 00:00:25,359
The faster number on the box is a profile stored on the stick itself.

7
00:00:25,639 --> 00:00:27,488
Intel calls it XMP.

8
00:00:27,768 --> 00:00:29,402
AMD calls it EXPO.

9
00:00:29,682 --> 00:00:34,401
It's a tested overclock, and your PC won't use it until you turn it on.

10
00:00:35,151 --> 00:00:41,080
Open your BIOS, find the XMP or EXPO setting, pick the profile, and save.

11
00:00:41,360 --> 00:00:43,965
Then check the memory speed in Task Manager.

12
00:00:44,715 --> 00:00:47,766
If your PC starts crashing, switch it back off.

13
00:00:48,046 --> 00:00:51,801
Full video: Every PC Component Explained, on the channel.

